---
import BlogLayout from '../../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'sonura', label: '1. Sonura' },
  { id: 'loudly', label: '2. Loudly' },
  { id: 'waves-illugen', label: '3. Waves ILLUGEN 2.0' },
  { id: 'splice', label: "4. Splice's sample library" },
  { id: 'beatoven', label: '5. Beatoven-style scoring tools' },
  { id: 'core-difference', label: 'Editable stems vs flat audio' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQ' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is the best AI sample generator in 2026?',
    answer: 'For producers working in a DAW, Sonura is the strongest all-rounder: it creates one-shots, loops, vocals and full multi-layer stems from a text prompt, in the browser or inside Ableton, FL Studio and Logic through the free Sonura Flow plugin. Waves ILLUGEN 2.0 is a good pick if you only need individual one-shots, loops and SFX with in-app key and length controls.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What file formats do AI sample generators export?',
    answer: 'Most export WAV for full quality and MP3 for quick previews. Sonura exports individual stems (drums, bass, melody, FX, vocals) as WAV on every plan, including free.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which AI sample generator works inside a DAW?',
    answer: 'Sonura runs inside your DAW as <a href="/plugin/">Sonura Flow</a>, a free-to-download VST3 and AU plugin for Ableton Live, FL Studio, Logic Pro and other hosts. Waves ILLUGEN is a standalone app you drag samples out of. Loudly and Splice are used from the browser or their own apps.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I use AI-generated samples commercially?',
    answer: 'It depends on the tool, so check each license. Sonura samples are royalty-free on every plan: free-plan creations are public domain (CC0), and on any paid plan they are exclusively yours.',
  },
];
---

<BlogLayout
  title="The 5 Best AI Sample Generators in 2026 (Compared)"
  description="A working producer's comparison of the best AI sample generators in 2026, from stem-based generation to browser tools and local plugins. What each is actually good for, and where editable stems matter."
  keywords="ai sample generator, sample generator, best ai sample generator, ai sample generators, ai sample maker, splice alternative"
  slug="best-ai-sample-generators-2026"
  publishedDate="2026-07-05"
  modifiedDate="2026-09-29"
  readTime="4 min read"
  toc={toc}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <div style="line-height: 1.8;">
    <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">Sample digging used to mean going through Splice or paying for a pack you never fully use. AI sample generators changed that, from local sound-design plugins to full browser-based instrument generators. Here's how the main options actually compare for a working producer.</p>

    <div style="overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 32px;">
      <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);">
            <th style="padding: 10px 12px 10px 0;">Tool</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px 12px;">What it creates</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px 12px;">Inside your DAW</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px 0 10px 12px;">Best for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody style="color: var(--muted);">
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light);"><td style="padding: 10px 12px 10px 0; color: var(--fg);">Sonura</td><td style="padding: 10px 12px;">One-shots, loops, vocals, full multi-layer stems</td><td style="padding: 10px 12px;">Yes, VST3 and AU plugin</td><td style="padding: 10px 0 10px 12px;">Producers who want editable, original material</td></tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light);"><td style="padding: 10px 12px 10px 0; color: var(--fg);">Loudly</td><td style="padding: 10px 12px;">Full background tracks</td><td style="padding: 10px 12px;">No, browser</td><td style="padding: 10px 0 10px 12px;">Content creators who need finished music fast</td></tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light);"><td style="padding: 10px 12px 10px 0; color: var(--fg);">Waves ILLUGEN 2.0</td><td style="padding: 10px 12px;">One-shots, loops, SFX, three WAVs per prompt</td><td style="padding: 10px 12px;">Standalone app, drag into DAW</td><td style="padding: 10px 0 10px 12px;">Single samples with key and length controls</td></tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light);"><td style="padding: 10px 12px 10px 0; color: var(--fg);">Splice</td><td style="padding: 10px 12px;">Search of a shared sample library</td><td style="padding: 10px 12px;">Desktop app and plugin</td><td style="padding: 10px 0 10px 12px;">Curated, recorded one-shots</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="padding: 10px 12px 10px 0; color: var(--fg);">Beatoven-style tools</td><td style="padding: 10px 12px;">Background scores for video</td><td style="padding: 10px 12px;">No, browser</td><td style="padding: 10px 0 10px 12px;">Video editors</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <section id="sonura" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 32px; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 24px; margin-top: 48px;">1. Sonura</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">Generates full multi-layer stems (drums, bass, melody, one-shots, and vocals) from a text prompt in seconds. Every export is an individual stem, not a flattened mix, so you can drop drums, bass, or melody straight into your session and build around them. It also runs inside Ableton Live, FL Studio and Logic Pro as <a href="/plugin/" class="text-link">Sonura Flow</a>, a free-to-download VST3 and AU plugin. Free to start, with exclusive commercial rights on every paid plan.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;"><strong>Best for:</strong> producers who want editable, original samples generated on demand rather than searched for.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="loudly" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 32px; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 24px; margin-top: 48px;">2. Loudly</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">Browser-based generator focused on full-track output for content and social use. Strong for quick background tracks, less built around stem-level editing for producers who want to rework individual elements.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;"><strong>Best for:</strong> creators who need finished background music fast and don't need to remix the parts.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="waves-illugen" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 32px; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 24px; margin-top: 48px;">3. Waves ILLUGEN 2.0</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">A standalone text-to-sound app from Waves. Type a prompt and it creates individual one-shots, loops, or SFX, three WAV files at a time, which you drag into your DAW. Version 2.0 added Lock to Key, loop-length and stereo controls. It does not create full multi-layer arrangements. See the full <a href="/blog/waves-illugen-vs-sonura/" class="text-link">Waves ILLUGEN vs Sonura comparison</a>.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;"><strong>Best for:</strong> producers who want individual samples with in-app musical control.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="splice" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 32px; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 24px; margin-top: 48px;">4. Splice's Sample Library (with Create tools)</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">Still primarily a search-and-browse sample library, with some AI-assisted discovery and creation layered in. You are mainly selecting from existing, shared sounds rather than generating original ones. See our <a href="/alternatives/splice-alternative/" class="text-link">Splice alternative breakdown</a> for the full comparison.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;"><strong>Best for:</strong> producers who want curated, professionally recorded one-shots and don't mind sharing sounds with other Splice users.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="beatoven" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 32px; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 24px; margin-top: 48px;">5. Beatoven-style AI scoring tools</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">Category of tools built for scoring video and content rather than producing standalone stems for a DAW session. Useful for a specific use case, less useful as a general-purpose <a href="/features/ai-loops/" class="text-link">sample generator</a> for music production.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;"><strong>Best for:</strong> video editors needing quick, royalty-free background scoring. See <a href="/use-cases/video-editors/" class="text-link">Sonura for video editors</a> for a producer-grade alternative.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="core-difference" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 32px; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 24px; margin-top: 48px;">The Core Difference: Editable Stems vs. Flat Audio</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">The single biggest split in this category is whether a tool hands you editable, multi-track material or a flattened audio file. If finishing a track in a DAW, stems matter more than almost anything else. They are the difference between actually producing and just picking a finished file off a shelf.</p>
    </section>

    <section style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div style="padding: 32px; margin-top: 48px; text-align: center;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.3; margin: 0 0 16px;">Create Original Samples, Not Shared Ones</h3>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 24px; color: var(--muted);">Create editable, multi-layer stems from a text prompt, with exclusive commercial rights on every paid plan.</p>
        <a href="https://app.sonurastudio.com/" class="button primary large"><span class="btn-label-full">Start Creating Free</span><span class="btn-label-short">Start free</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BlogLayout>
